meta {
  name: Confirm Meta Campaign Type
  type: http
  seq: 16
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/meta/campaigns/{{metaCampaignId}}?tenant_id={{tenantId}}&dry_run=true
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "campaign_type_id": "{{campaignTypeId}}",
    "learn_keyword": false
  }
}

docs {
  Confirm or correct a campaign -> type mapping, then re-route the leads that
  campaign already has.

  Writes campaign_type_id, mapping_status='confirmed', confirmed_by and
  confirmed_at, then calls leads-service POST /internal/campaign-reclassify and
  returns its counts (leads relabelled, reassigned, left unassigned, by branch).

  learn_keyword: appends a token taken from THIS campaign's name to the type's
  match_keywords, so the next similarly-named campaign auto-matches. Opt-in on
  purpose — 'HIR_Gurugram_Trainer_Sep26' offers 'gurugram' as readily as
  'trainer', and a wrong keyword silently mistypes every future campaign
  containing it.

  dry_run=true RETURNS THE PREVIEW AND WRITES NOTHING — not the mapping, not the
  keyword, not the reclassification.

  Regression checks:
    * dry_run=true  -> impact counts returned; the ext.meta_campaigns row and
      every lead are provably unchanged afterwards. Re-run List-Meta-Campaigns
      and confirm mapping_status is still what it was.
    * dry_run=false -> mapping written, then leads fan out.
    * `?dry_run=false` must actually WRITE. It is parsed as a real boolean, not
      via z.coerce.boolean(), under which the string "false" is truthy and the
      Confirm button would silently do nothing.
    * a campaign belonging to another tenant -> 404, never 403: distinguishing
      the two would confirm that tenant's campaign exists.
    * a campaign_type_id from another tenant -> 400
    * caller below super_admin               -> 403
}
